{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>A wandering Aramean was my father.</b> He called Jacob an Aramean, and the construal of the verse is 'an Aramean was my father,' and the meaning of אובד is 'poor' — this is the view of R. Abraham ibn Ezra, of blessed memory, and not like the view of the Rabbis, of blessed memory. And it says: when my father was in Aram he was אובד, that is to say, he was poor, without money; and so too (Proverbs 31:6): ״תנו שכר לאובד״ — for so we find that he had great suffering in his labor for Laban, as he said (Genesis 31:40): ״הייתי ביום אכלני חורב וגו׳״." "<b>And he went down to Egypt.</b> That is to say, he was אובד when he went down to Egypt, for I inherited nothing from him; and he was also there a sojourner, few in number, and afterward he became there a great nation. And so the children were, as a whole, Israel: You, YHWH, brought us out of harsh bondage and redeemed us with signs and wonders, and gave us a land flowing with milk and honey. And the intention in the rhetorical passage of this pericah is that a person is obliged to take to heart, at the time of his ascent and tranquility, the time of decline and lowliness that he had; and so Qohelet said (Ecclesiastes 7:14): ״ביום טובה היה בטוב וביום רעה ראה״ — its meaning being, on the day of good, look upon the day of evil; and this is so that he may reflect upon the advantage he has, and then give thanks for it to HaShem, may He be exalted, who is good and does good to him." "<b>And the view</b> of Onqelos regarding the word אובד is that it refers back to Laban, for Laban sought to destroy Jacob our father, had not the word of HaShem come to him and said to him (Genesis 31:29): ״השמר לך מדבר עם יעקב מטוב עד רע״; and even though he did not destroy him, since he sought to destroy him, Scripture accounts it against him as though he had destroyed him. And this is what the Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded: if an idolater intended to commit a transgression and did not do it, the Holy One, blessed be He, accounts it to him as though he did it, as it is said: ארמי אובד אבי — and where did Laban destroy Jacob? Rather, because he intended to do it, Scripture accounts it against him as though he did it. But if an idolater intended to perform a commandment and did not do it, it is not recorded for him until he does it, as it is said (Daniel 6:15): ״ועד מעלי שמשא הוה משתדר להצלותיה״ — and the Holy One, blessed be He, did not account it to him until he did it. But Israel, if he intended to commit a transgression and did not do it, the Holy One, blessed be He, does not record it until he does it, as it is written (Micah 2:1): ״הוי חושבי און ופועלי רע על משכבותם״; David said (Psalms 66:18): ״און אם ראיתי בלבי לא ישמע ה׳״. And if he intended to perform a commandment, even though he did not do it, Scripture accounts it to him as though he did it, for David intended to build the Holy Temple, and even though he did not build it, it is recorded in his name, as it is written (Psalms 30): ״מזמור שיר חנכת הבית לדוד״ — concerning Solomon it is not said, but concerning David; so they expounded in Midrash Tehillim on the psalm, A song at the dedication of the House."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:51:16.669038632Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}